course: dictionary
lesson: 10638
title: Unhinge: doors, minds and myths
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Unhinge» comes from «hinge», the metal joint that lets a door swing. To unhinge something is to take it off its hinges. Used another way, it means to upset someone so much that they stop thinking clearly. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer wants.
explain: Look at this sentence: «Snakes swallow food whole; the two halves of the lower jaw are joined by stretchy tissue, so the idea that they unhinge their jaws is a myth.» A myth is a popular idea that is not true. So here, unhinge means taking the jaw apart like a door off its hinges, and the writer says snakes do not do that.
explain: In everyday talk, «unhinged» can describe a person or a video that is wildly silly, strange or crazy. Stories use it for villains who have lost control. The clues are always in the surrounding words, so check them before you decide.
fact: «Unhinge» can mean to take a door or lid off its hinges.
fact: «Unhinge» can also mean to upset someone so much that they cannot think clearly or calmly.
fact: «Unhinged» means acting in a wild, strange or crazy way.
fact: Snakes do not «unhinge» their jaws. Their lower jaw is in two halves joined by stretchy tissue, so the jaws stretch.
fact: A myth is a popular belief that is not true, and the snake idea is one.
exercise: «Snakes do not unhinge their jaws, but people often think they do.» What does unhinge mean in the first sense, the one used for doors? | *To take a door off its hinges | To open a window wide | To paint a door a bright colour | To shut a door very loudly
exercise: Snakes swallow prey whole, and the idea that they unhinge their jaws is a myth. What is a myth? | *A popular idea that is not true | A type of snake | A true fact from a science book | A bone in the jaw
exercise: Ella says a video is «unhinged». What does she mean? | It is calm, quiet and very old | *It is wildly silly, strange or crazy | It is made of wood | It is about a door
test: The loud news unhinged her, and she could not think clearly for an hour. What does unhinged mean here? | Fast asleep | Fixed firmly in place | *Upset so much that she could not think clearly | Happy and calm
test: Which word is the verb in this sentence? «The cook unhinged the cupboard door to paint it.» | cook | paint | cupboard | *unhinged
exam: Is it true that a snake unhinges its jaw to swallow an egg? | No, snakes never eat eggs | Yes, but only at night | *No, the lower jaw halves are joined by stretchy tissue, so the jaws stretch | Yes, both halves of the jaw swing wide open
